      "title": "Learning Doc",
      "content": {
        "doc_md": "The HTML page contains a <div> section and a <button>.\n\nThe <div> \nsection is used to display information from a server.\n\nThe <button> calls a function (if it is clicked).\n\nThe function requests data from a web \nserver and displays it:\n\nAJAX =AsynchronousJavaScriptAndXML.\n\nAJAX is not a programming language.\n\nAJAX just uses a combination of:\n\nAJAX allows web pages to be updated asynchronously by exchanging data with a web server behind the scenes.\nThis means that it is possible to update parts of a web page, without reloading the whole page.\n\nModern Browsers can use Fetch API instead of the XMLHttpRequest Object.\n\nThe Fetch API interface allows web browser to make HTTP requests to web servers.\n\nIf you use the XMLHttpRequest Object, Fetch can do the same in a simpler way.\n\n### Code Snippets\n\n```\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n<body>\n<div id=\"demo\">\n<h2>Let AJAX change this text</h2>\n<button type=\"button\" onclick=\"loadDoc()\">Change Content</button>\n</div>\n</body>\n</html>\n\n---\n\nfunction loadDoc() {\nconst xhttp = new XMLHttpRequest();\nxhttp.onload = function() {\ndocument.getElementById(\"demo\").innerHTML = this.responseText;\n}\nxhttp.open(\"GET\", \"ajax_info.txt\", true);\nxhttp.send();\n}\n```"
      }
